; Text may fill a hole in syntax. Text may never become structure.
;
; The rule that lets `eval` exist without ending cost readability. A model is
; asked for something that looks exactly like a Rebis program; the answer is
; bound and then called. It does NOT run — it is text, and text is not
; structure, so the call is an undefined macro and the run says so.
;
; The proof is the COUNT plus the diagnostic. If the answer had been executed
; there would be a third call and no diagnostic; both hold, from two directions,
; and a text-generating model can fake neither.
;
; (There is deliberately no `absent` check. The marker string has to be written
; into the prompt in order to ask for it, so it appears in the log whatever
; happens — an expectation that can never pass is worse than none.)
; expect: calls 2
; expect: diagnostic undefined macro
; expect: answers
(= answer ("Reply with exactly this and nothing else: (\"SHOULD-NOT-RUN\")")
   ((answer)
    "Reply with exactly the word: bounded"))
